DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddiigg command is a flexible tool for interrogating DNS name servers.
     It performs DNS lookups and displays the answers that are returned from
     the name servers that were queried.  Although ddiigg is normally used with
     command line arguments, it also has a batch mode of operation for reading
     lookup requests from a file.  When no command line arguments or options
     are given, ddiigg will perform an NS query for '.' (the root).

     A typical invocation of ddiigg looks like:

           dig @server [options] name type [class] [+queryopt]

     @_s_e_r_v_e_r    The name or IP address of the name server to query.  When the
                argument is a hostname, ddiigg resolves that name before querying
                that name server.  If no _s_e_r_v_e_r argument is provided, ddiigg will
                try each of the servers listed in resolv.conf(5).  If no us‐
                able addresses are found, ddiigg will send the query to the local
                host.  The reply from the name server that responds is dis‐
                played.

     _n_a_m_e       The name of the resource record that is to be looked up.

     _t_y_p_e       The type of query, as documented for --tt.  The default is AA.

     _c_l_a_s_s      The query class, as documented for --cc.  The default is IINN.

     +_q_u_e_r_y_o_p_t  One or more query options, as documented in _Q_U_E_R_Y _O_P_T_I_O_N_S, be‐
                low.

     The options are as follows:

     --44      Use IPv4 only.

     --66      Use IPv6 only.

     --bb _s_o_u_r_c_e_a_d_d_r[#_p_o_r_t]
             Set the source IP address of the query, which is useful on ma‐
             chines with multiple interfaces.  The _s_o_u_r_c_e_a_d_d_r must be a valid
             address on one of the host's network interfaces, or "0.0.0.0" or
             "::".  An optional port may be specified by appending "#<port>".

     --cc _c_l_a_s_s
             Set the query class.  The default is IINN; other classes are HHSS for
             Hesiod records and CCHH for Chaosnet records.

     --ff _f_i_l_e
             Batch mode: ddiigg reads a list of lookup requests to process from
             the given _f_i_l_e.  Each line in the file should be organized in the
             same way they would be presented as queries to ddiigg using the com‐
             mand-line interface.

     --hh      Display a brief summary of command line arguments and options.

     --ii      Do reverse IPv6 lookups using the obsolete RFC 1886 IP6.INT do‐
             main, which is no longer in use.  Obsolete bit string label
             queries (RFC 2874) are not attempted.

     --kk _k_e_y_f_i_l_e
             Sign queries using TSIG.  The format of the _k_e_y_f_i_l_e is as fol‐
             lows:

                   key "keyname" {
                           algorithm hmac;
                           secret "base64-secret";
                   };

             _k_e_y_n_a_m_e is the name of the key, and _b_a_s_e_6_4_-_s_e_c_r_e_t is the
             base64-encoded shared secret.  _h_m_a_c is the name of the key algo‐
             rithm; valid choices are hhmmaacc--sshhaa11, hhmmaacc--sshhaa222244, hhmmaacc--sshhaa225566,
             hhmmaacc--sshhaa338844, and hhmmaacc--sshhaa551122.

     --pp _p_o_r_t
             Send the query to a non-standard port on the server, instead of
             the default port 53.  This option would be used to test a name
             server that has been configured to listen for queries on a non-
             standard port number.

     --qq _n_a_m_e
             The domain name to query.  This is useful to distinguish the _n_a_m_e
             from other arguments.

     --tt _t_y_p_e
             The resource record type to query.  It can be any valid query
             type.  If it is a resource record type supported in BIND 9, it
             can be given by the type mnemonic (such as NNSS or AAAAAAAA).  The de‐
             fault query type is AA, unless the --xx option is supplied to indi‐
             cate a reverse lookup.  A zone transfer can be requested by spec‐
             ifying a type of AAXXFFRR.  When an incremental zone transfer is re‐
             quired, set the _t_y_p_e to IIXXFFRR=_N.  The incremental zone transfer
             will contain the changes made to the zone since the serial number
             in the zone's SOA record was _N.

             All resource record types can be expressed as TTYYPPEE_n_n, where _n_n is
             the number of the type.  If the resource record type is not sup‐
             ported in BIND 9, the result will be displayed as described in
             RFC 3597.

     --uu      Print query times in microseconds instead of milliseconds.

     --vv      Print the version number and exit.

     --xx _a_d_d_r
             Simplified reverse lookups, for mapping addresses to names.  The
             _a_d_d_r is an IPv4 address in dotted-decimal notation, or a colon-
             delimited IPv6 address.  When the --xx is used, there is no need to
             provide the _n_a_m_e, _c_l_a_s_s and _t_y_p_e arguments.  ddiigg automatically
             performs a lookup for a name like ‘94.2.0.192.in-addr.arpa’ and
             sets the query type and class to PPTTRR and IINN respectively.  IPv6
             addresses are looked up using nibble format under the IP6.ARPA
             domain (but see also the --ii option).

     --yy [_h_m_a_c:]_k_e_y_n_a_m_e:_s_e_c_r_e_t
             Sign queries using TSIG with the given authentication key.
             _k_e_y_n_a_m_e is the name of the key, and _s_e_c_r_e_t is the base64 encoded
             shared secret.  _h_m_a_c is the name of the key algorithm; valid
             choices are hhmmaacc--sshhaa11, hhmmaacc--sshhaa222244, hhmmaacc--sshhaa225566, hhmmaacc--sshhaa338844, and
             hhmmaacc--sshhaa551122.  If _h_m_a_c is not specified, the default is
             hhmmaacc--sshhaa225566.

             NOTE: You should use the --kk option and avoid the --yy option, be‐
             cause with --yy the shared secret is supplied as a command line ar‐
             gument in clear text.  This may be visible in the output from
             ps(1) or in a history file maintained by the user's shell.

     The IINN and CCHH class names overlap with the IN and CH top level domain
     names.  Either use the --tt and --cc options to specify the type and class,
     use the --qq to specify the domain name, or use "IN." and "CH." when look‐
     ing up these top level domains.

QQUUEERRYY OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     ddiigg provides a number of query options which affect the way in which
     lookups are made and the results displayed.  Some of these set or reset
     flag bits in the query header, some determine which sections of the an‐
     swer get printed, and others determine the timeout and retry strategies.

     Each query option is identified by a keyword preceded by a plus sign (++).
     Some keywords set or reset an option.  These may be preceded by the
     string nnoo to negate the meaning of that keyword.  Other keywords assign
     values to options like the timeout interval.  They have the form
     ++_k_e_y_w_o_r_d=_v_a_l_u_e.  Keywords may be abbreviated, provided the abbreviation
     is unambiguous; for example, ++ccdd is equivalent to ++ccddffllaagg.  The query op‐
     tions are:

     ++[nnoo]aaaaffllaagg
             A synonym for ++[nnoo]aaaaoonnllyy.

     ++[nnoo]aaaaoonnllyy
             Set the "aa" flag in the query (off by default).

     ++[nnoo]aaddddiittiioonnaall
             Display the additional section of a reply (on by default).

     ++[nnoo]aaddffllaagg
             Set the AD (authentic data) bit in the query (on by default).
             This requests the server to return whether all of the answer and
             authority sections have all been validated as secure according to
             the security policy of the server.  AD=1 indicates that all
             records have been validated as secure and the answer is not from
             an OPT-OUT range.  AD=0 indicates that some part of the answer
             was insecure or not validated.

     ++[nnoo]aallll
             Set or clear all display flags.

     ++[nnoo]aannsswweerr
             Display the answer section of a reply (on by default).

     ++[nnoo]aauutthhoorriittyy
             Display the authority section of a reply (on by default).

     ++[nnoo]bbeesstteeffffoorrtt
             Attempt to display the contents of messages which are malformed
             (on by default).

     ++bbuuffssiizzee=_#
             Set the UDP message buffer size advertised using EDNS0 to _#
             bytes.  The maximum and minimum sizes of this buffer are 65535
             and 0 respectively.  Values outside this range are rounded up or
             down appropriately.  Values other than zero will cause an EDNS
             query to be sent.

     ++[nnoo]ccddffllaagg
             Set the CD (checking disabled) bit in the query (off by default).
             This requests the server to not perform DNSSEC validation of re‐
             sponses.

     ++[nnoo]ccllaassss
             Display the CLASS when printing the record (on by default).

     ++[nnoo]ccmmdd
             Print an initial comment identifying the version of ddiigg and the
             query options that have been applied (on by default).

     ++[nnoo]ccoommmmeennttss
             Display comment lines in the output (on by default).

     ++[nnoo]ccooookkiiee[=_v_a_l_u_e]
             Send a COOKIE EDNS option, containing an optional _v_a_l_u_e (off by
             default).  Replaying a COOKIE from a previous response will allow
             the server to identify a previous client.

             ++ccooookkiiee is automatically set when ++ttrraaccee is in use, to better em‐
             ulate the default queries from a name server.

             This option was formerly called ++[nnoo]ssiitt (Server Identity Token).
             In BIND 9.10.0 through BIND 9.10.2, it sent the experimental op‐
             tion code 65001.  This was changed to option code 10 in BIND
             9.10.3 when the DNS COOKIE option was allocated.

             The ++[nnoo]ssiitt option is now deprecated, but has been retained as a
             synonym for ++[nnoo]ccooookkiiee for backward compatibility within the
             BIND 9.10 branch.

     ++[nnoo]ccrryyppttoo
             Display cryptographic fields in DNSSEC records (on by default).
             The contents of these field are unnecessary to debug most DNSSEC
             validation failures and removing them makes it easier to see the
             common failures.  When omitted they are replaced by the string
             "[omitted]" or in the DNSKEY case the key id is displayed as the
             replacement, e.g. "[ key id = value ]".

     ++[nnoo]ddeeffnnaammee
             Deprecated, treated as a synonym for ++[nnoo]sseeaarrcchh.

     ++[nnoo]ddnnsssseecc
             Request DNSSEC records be sent by setting the DNSSEC OK bit (DO)
             in the OPT record in the additional section of the query (off by
             default).

     ++ddoommaaiinn=_n_a_m_e
             Set the search list to contain the single domain _n_a_m_e, as if
             specified in a ddoommaaiinn directive in resolv.conf(5), and enable
             search list processing as if the ++sseeaarrcchh option were given (off
             by default).

     ++[nnoo]eeddnnss[=_#]
             Use EDNS in the query (on by default).  A version may also be
             specified, from 0 (the default) to 255.  ++nnooeeddnnss disables EDNS
             and clears the remembered version.

     ++eeddnnssffllaaggss[=_#]
             Set the must-be-zero EDNS flags bits (Z bits) to the specified
             value (0 by default).  Decimal, hex and octal encodings are ac‐
             cepted.  Setting a named flag (e.g. DO) will silently be ignored.

     ++[nnoo]eeddnnssnneeggoottiiaattiioonn
             Enable EDNS version negotiation (off by default).

     ++[nnoo]eeddnnssoopptt[=_c_o_d_e[:_v_a_l_u_e]]
             Specify EDNS option with code point _c_o_d_e and optionally payload
             of _v_a_l_u_e as a hexadecimal string.  _c_o_d_e can be either an EDNS op‐
             tion name (for example, NNSSIIDD or EECCSS), or an arbitrary numeric
             value.  ++nnooeeddnnssoopptt clears the EDNS options to be sent.

     ++[nnoo]eexxppiirree
             Send an EDNS Expire option (off by default).

     ++[nnoo]ffaaiill
             Do not try the next server if you receive a SERVFAIL.  This op‐
             tion is on by default, which is the reverse of normal stub re‐
             solver behavior.

     ++[nnoo]iiddeennttiiffyy
             Show the IP address and port number that supplied the answer (off
             by default).  This option has no effect unless the ++sshhoorrtt option
             is enabled.

     ++[nnoo]iiddnnoouutt
             Convert puny code on output.  This version of ddiigg does not sup‐
             port IDN.

     ++[nnoo]iiggnnoorree
             Ignore truncation in UDP responses.  This option is off by de‐
             fault, which means truncated responses cause retrying with TCP.

     ++[nnoo]kkeeeeppooppeenn
             Keep the TCP socket open between queries and reuse it.  This op‐
             tion is off by default, which means that a new TCP socket is cre‐
             ated for each lookup.

     ++[nnoo]mmuullttiilliinnee
             Print records like the SOA records in a verbose multi-line format
             with human-readable comments.  This option is off by default,
             which means that each record is printed on a single line to fa‐
             cilitate machine parsing of the ddiigg output.

     ++nnddoottss=_#
             Set the number of dots that have to appear in _n_a_m_e to _# for it to
             be considered absolute.  The default value is that defined using
             the ndots statement in resolv.conf(5), or 1 if no ndots statement
             is present.  Names with fewer dots are interpreted as relative
             names and will be searched for in the domains listed in the
             sseeaarrcchh or ddoommaaiinn directive in resolv.conf(5) if ++sseeaarrcchh is set.

     ++[nnoo]nnssiidd
             Include an EDNS name server ID request when sending a query (off
             by default).

     ++[nnoo]nnsssseeaarrcchh
             Attempt to find the authoritative name servers for the zone con‐
             taining the name being looked up and display the SOA record that
             each name server has for the zone (off by default).

     ++[nnoo]oonneessooaa
             Print only one (starting) SOA record when performing an AAXXFFRR.
             This option is off by default, which means that both the starting
             and the ending SOA records are printed.

     ++[nnoo]ooppccooddee=_#
             Set or restore the DNS message opcode to the specified value,
             which can be QQUUEERRYY (the default), IIQQUUEERRYY, SSTTAATTUUSS, NNOOTTIIFFYY, UUPPDDAATTEE,
             or an integer number in the range from 0 to 15.

     ++[nnoo]qqrr
             Print the query as it is sent (off by default).

     ++[nnoo]qquueessttiioonn
             Print the question section of a query as a comment when an answer
             is returned (on by default).

     ++[nnoo]rrddffllaagg
             A synonym for ++[nnoo]rreeccuurrssee.

     ++[nnoo]rreeccuurrssee
             Set the RD (recursion desired) bit in the query (on by default).
             Recursion is automatically disabled when the ++nnsssseeaarrcchh or ++ttrraaccee
